  "abstract": "<jats:p>Direct laser acceleration can generate high-current relativistic electron beams, yet their intrinsically large divergence severely limits beam transport and subsequent applications. In this work, a compact collimation scheme for direct-laser-accelerated electron beams is proposed and numerically investigated. By placing a wedge above the gas jet target, the gas flow is modified to form a millimeter-scale plasma region downstream of the jet, which acts as an integrated plasma lens for the accelerated electron beam. Numerical results show that, after passing through this plasma-lens region, the electron beam divergence can be reduced by approximately a factor of two. Owing to its simple configuration and ease of implementation, this scheme offers an effective route to improve the transport properties of electron beams without requiring complex target fabrication or stringent alignment. These results demonstrate that wedge-modified gas jet targets are promising for manipulating and applying electron beams in compact radiation sources and advanced accelerator systems.</jats:p>",
